00:00:08.532 --> 00:00:09.740
HEATHER CROSS: Welcome back--

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.480
Heather, again.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.980
EVAN JONES: And
hello from me, Evan.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.690
I'm a technical curriculum
developer for Google Cloud.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.250
If you're a
data-driven enterprise,

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.560
you've likely be met with
an explosion of data growth

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.520
in recent years.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.010
You need to find the best way
to store all those terabytes

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.790
and petabytes at scale.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:24.790
So welcome to episode 3.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.664
And here's where we're
going to introduce you

00:00:26.664 --> 00:00:28.190
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.230
It's a storage solution
specifically suited

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.930
for your volumes of both
structured and unstructured

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.292
data.

00:00:34.292 --> 00:00:37.268
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.710
HEATHER CROSS: Data doesn't
just mean rows and columns

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.030
of numbers anymore.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.710
You may want to start
unstructured data

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.170
like messages, documents,
multimedia, files,

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.130
web pages, and so on.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:49.920
Whatever your storage
requirements are,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.710
security, availability and
cost will be your three

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.210
key considerations.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.655
Cloud Storage aims to meet
all these requirements.

00:00:56.655 --> 00:00:58.530
EVAN JONES: At its core,
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.790
is a massively scalable
storage solution.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.430
And it runs in the
exact same backend

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.120
that powers Google's own
applications like storing

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.860
Gmail's email messages, the
billions of high-res Google

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.410
Photos, and all the data that's
associated with searching

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.659
and indexing the internet.

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:13.950
So how do you know which type
of storage option for you

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.038
to choose from?

00:01:15.038 --> 00:01:17.428
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:19.947 --> 00:01:22.280
HEATHER CROSS: Cloud Storage
offers four different kinds

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.820
of storage,
Multi-Regional, Regional,

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.570
Nearline, and Coldline.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.062
You can check them out
in more detail here.

00:01:29.062 --> 00:01:30.770
EVAN JONES: So let's
take a few examples.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.560
Say you wanted to store disaster
recovery backups you'd rarely

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.160
need, say, once a year.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.887
Then you would consider
Coldline Storage.

00:01:36.887 --> 00:01:38.720
You'll get a lower cost
per gigabyte stored,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.300
since you'll be accessing
your data as frequently.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.960
Way on the other
side of the spectrum,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.320
say you wanted to support
streaming data like videos,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.080
games, or your applications.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.382
Here you want to optimize
for high global uptime,

00:01:49.382 --> 00:01:51.590
so you want to consider
something like Multi-Regional

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.072
or Regional Storage instead.

00:01:53.072 --> 00:01:55.280
HEATHER CROSS: The three
key factors you want to keep

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.010
in mind when choosing a storage
option is availability--

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.040
or how frequently you
need to access the data--

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.680
minimum storage duration--
so asking yourself,

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.270
are you archiving
monthly backups,

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.160
or storing short-lived data?

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.639
And finally, pay-per-use
pricing for storage and access.

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:12.560
Whichever option
you choose, they all

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.830
offer the same throughput,
low latency, high durability

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.969
and security.

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:19.364
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.130
EVAN JONES: Securing your
data is always top of mind.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.350
All applications and data
on Google Cloud Storage

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.060
benefit from the
same security model

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.910
that Google itself uses to
keep its own customers safe

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.019
when they use Gmail, Search,
or other Google Apps.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:35.560
HEATHER CROSS: Data encryption
takes place on the server side

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.930
as soon as the data is
received before it's even

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.655
written to the disk and stored.

00:02:39.655 --> 00:02:41.530
You can also provide
your own encryption keys

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.775
for server-side encryption.

00:02:42.775 --> 00:02:44.150
EVAN JONES: Google
Cloud Platform

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.566
and Google's own infrastructure
is certified for a growing

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:49.510
number of compliance standards
and controls and undergoes

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:52.150
several independent,
third-party security audits.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.590
HEATHER CROSS: You can
refer to our first episode

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.923
for more information on the
management of access control

00:02:55.923 --> 00:02:58.212
using Cloud IAM.

00:02:58.212 --> 00:03:01.188
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:02.195 --> 00:03:04.570
EVAN JONES: Everything that
you store in Cloud Storage is

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.257
contained inside of a bucket.

00:03:06.257 --> 00:03:08.590
Buckets are these elastic
containers that hold your data

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.690
and the metadata that's
associated with them,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.516
and they help you organize and
control access to your data.

00:03:13.516 --> 00:03:15.640
HEATHER CROSS: The Google
Cloud Platform Console UI

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.750
can be used to perform
simple storage management

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.170
tasks for Cloud Storage,
including the creation

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.760
and management of buckets.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.350
This and more
advanced tasks can be

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.754
performed using the
gsutil Command-line Tool.

